%*+-------------------------------------+-----------------+---------------------+
!           Cursor Movement           !      Block      !        Misc.        !
+-------------------------------------+-----------------+---------------------+
! ^S    Char left   ^D    Char right  ! ^K^B  Start     ! ^J    Help          !
! ^A    Word left   ^F    Word right  ! ^K^K  End       ! ^K^D  Exit editor   !
! ^Q^S  Line start  ^Q^D  Line end    ! ^K^Y  Delete    ! ^K^X  Abort editor  !
! ^E    Line up     ^X    Line down   ! ^K^C  Copy      ! ^V    INSERT flag   !
! ^Q^E  Page top    ^Q^X  Page bottom ! ^K^V  Move      ! ^Z    AUTO flag     !
! ^R    Page up     ^C    Page down   ! ^K^P  Print     ! ^B    TABS flag     !
! ^Q^R  Start text  ^Q^C  End text    ! ^K^R  Read      ! ^W    ADJUST flag   !
!                                     ! ^K^W  Write     ! ^P    Control char  !
! Also use the keyboard cursor arrows ! ^K^H  Unmark    !                     !
+-------------------------------------+-----------------+---------------------+
!               Delete                !      Edit       !        Search       !
+-------------------------------------+-----------------+---------------------+
! DEL   Char left   ^G    Char right  ! RETN  New line  ! ^Q^F  Find string   !
! ^QDEL Line start  ^Q^Y  Line end    ! ^I    Tabulate  ! ^Q^A  Search/repl   !
! ^Y    Whole line  ^T    Word right  ! ^N    Insert CR ! ^L    Repeat        !
+-------------------------------------+-----------------+---------------------+
! Find/repl options: G=Global, N=Don't ask, U=Case insensitive, W=Words only  !
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

% PolyPascal On-Line Help: Command Lines.

When PolyPascal is ready to receive and execute a command line it prints
a double angle bracket ('>>').  The following commands are available:

LOAD     Read source text from disk.   SAVE     Save source text on disk.
NAME     Show/edit current filename.   EDIT     Start the editor.
WHERE    Restart the editor.           COMPILE  Compile source text.
RUN      Start execution of program    PROGRAM  Compile to program file.
OBJECT   Compile to object file.       FIND     Find runtime error line.
DIR      Show disk directory.          USE      Show/edit disk/user.
MEMORY   Show memory parameters.       ZAP      Clear source text.
HELP     Show help text.               QUIT     Exit from PolyPascal.

Note that all commands can be abbreviated to their first letter, for
example 'L' instead of 'LOAD'.

If you want additional help with one of the commands, then type 'H' or
'HELP' followed by the command (or its first letter).

%LPolyPascal On-Line Help: LOAD command.

The LOAD command is used to read a new source text file into memory.
The format of the command line is:

                             LOAD <filename>

The system automatically selects '.PAS' if the extension is omitted.  If
the is omitted,  the work file's name is used. If the work file has been
modified since it was loaded or last saved, PolyPascal asks if it should
be saved before  the new file is loaded.  If you answer no,  the changes
made will be lost.  When a file is loaded,  it is designated as the work
file, and its name is automatically used by the LOAD, SAVE, PROGRAM, and
OBJECT  commands when nothing  else is specified.  If the specified file
does not exist, PolyPascal prints 'No such file'. If there is not enough
room in  memory to load the file,  PolyPascal prints 'File too big'.  In
both  of these cases,  memory is cleared and the work file  name becomes
'WORK.PAS'.

%SPolyPascal On-Line Help: SAVE command.

The SAVE command is used to store the work file on disk. The format of
the command line is:

                             SAVE <filename>

The system automatically selects '.PAS' if the extension is omitted.  If
the is omitted, the work file's name is used. If a file of the same name
and  extension already  exists on the disk,  this file's  type  will  be
changed  to  '.BAK'  before the new file is created.  If the filename is
illegal, PolyPascal  prints 'Invalid filename'. If the disk directory is
full,  PolyPascal  prints  'Directory is full'.  If  the  disk  is full,
PolyPascal  prints  'Disk is full'.  If  one  of  the  above  errors  is
reported,  insert another disk, execute a USE command, and then try SAVE
again.

%NPolyPascal On-Line Help: NAME command.

NAME  the command is used to show and  optionally change the work file's
name.  The work file's name  is used  by the LOAD,  SAVE,  PROGRAM,  and
OBJECT commands if nothing else is specified.  The format of the command
line is:

                             NAME <filename>

The system automatically selects '.PAS' if the extension is omitted.  If
the is omitted,  the work file's name is simply printed; otherwise,  the
work file's name is changed to <filename>.

The work file's name is set to 'WORK.PAS' when starting PolyPascal,  and
when a ZAP command is executed.

%EPolyPascal On-Line Help: EDIT command.

EDIT command starts the editor. The format of the command line is:

                             EDIT <filename>

The system automatically selects '.PAS' if the extension is omitted.  If
the is  omitted  (which it normally is),  the editor starts immediately,
and you can then  edit the work file.  If  <filename>  is specified, the
work file is saved (if it has been modified)  and the new file is loaded
before  the editor starts.  If in this  case you exit the editor  with a
^K^D command,  PolyPascal performs the reverse process,  i.e., saves the
new file and reloads the original work file.  If, on the other hand, you
exit  the editor with a ^K^X command,  the new file is designated as the
work file.

%WPolyPascal On-Line Help: WHERE command.

WHERE  is the  command  used to restart the editor.  When  the editor is
exited (with a ^K^D or ^K^X command),  the system remembers the cursor's
position in the text.  If you later execute a WHERE command,  the editor
is restarted and the cursor is moved to the place you previously left.

When the compiler reports an error, it also ensures that a WHERE command
will move the cursor  to the location of the error.  After a compilation
with  errors,  a  WHERE  command  will  therefore  start  the editor and
position the cursor at the error.

%CPolyPascal On-Line Help: COMPILE command.

When the compiler is started from a COMPILE command,  the object code is
stored directly in memory, immediately following the source text.

After  compilation  is complete (provided that no error was found),  the
system  informs you of  the size of the code segment,  the data segment,
and the free memory (stack segment):

                  Code: rrrrrr bytes (aaaa paragraphs)
                  Data: ssssss bytes (bbbb paragraphs)
                  Free: tttttt bytes (cccc paragraphs)

The numbers in parentheses  are the paragraph size  (in hex notation) of
the relevant area. A paragraph corresponds to 16 bytes.

Note that when the editor is used, any object code will be deleted.

%RPolyPascal On-Line Help: RUN command.

The RUN command starts a program. The format of the command line is:

                              RUN <params>

where is an optional parameter list passed to the program in its command
line  buffer.  If no object code  exists when the command is given,  the
compiler is started to translate the source text (see the 'HELP COMPILE'
text).  Provided that no  error was found during compilation,  or if the
object  code is already present,  the program is started.  When it ends,
control is automatically handed over to the PolyPascal command level.

If a runtime error occurs during program execution, it will be
stopped, and an error message is printed:

                     EXECUTION ERROR 04 AT PC=254E
                     Program terminated

The FIND command can then be used  to find the statement that caused the
error (see the 'HELP FIND' text).

%PPolyPascal On-Line Help: PROGRAM command.

When the compiler is started from a PROGRAM command,  the program code is
stored in a disk file. The format of the command line is:

       PROGRAM <filename>,<ssegmin>,<ssegmax>,<csegmin>,<dsegmin>

If is omitted, the current filename is chosen with the extension changed
to '.CMD'.  <ssegmin>,  <ssegmax>,  <csegmin>,  and  <dsegmin>  are  hex
numbers (up to four digits).  Each of these  parameters can be specified
or omitted  at will,  for example 'PROGRAM TEST,800' (only <ssegmin>) or
'PROGRAM TEST,,,CD8,12E4' (only <csegmin> and <dsegmin>).

<ssegmin>  and  <ssegmax>  specify  the  minimum  and  maximum sizes (in
paragraphs) of the stack segment.  If <ssegmin> is omitted,  100 hex (4K
bytes) is selected.  If <ssegmax> is omitted,  the value of {ssegmin} is
selected.  <csegmin> and <csegmax> specify the minimum sizes of the code
and data segments.   If they are omitted, the lowest possible values are
selected.  The values must not be  larger than FFF hex (64K bytes),  and
they are normally  only specified in connection with programs that chain
other programs with larger code and/or data segments.  After compilation
is complete, the program can be started simply by typing its name.

%OPolyPascal On-Line Help: OBJECT command.

OBJECT command  corresponds to the PROGRAM command,  with  the exception
that  the run-time program portion is not included in the disk file that
is generated by the compiler.  The format of the command line is:

                            OBJECT <filename>

If  <filename> is omitted,  the current filename is chosen with its type
changed to '.CHN' (short for chain).  Object files  can only be  started
by  a  call to the  chain  standard  procedure from  another  PolyPascal
program file.  Further description can be found in the PolyPascal
Programming Manual.

%FPolyPascal On-Line Help: FIND command.

FIND  command  is  used to  locate a  statement  in the source text that
corresponds to an offset address in the program code.  The format of the
command line is:

                             FIND <offset>

where <offset> is the address in the program code of  the statement that
is to be found.  The address must be given in  hex notation  (up to four
hex digits).  If <offset> is omitted,  the system automatically  selects
the  address of the last run-time error.  Thus, simply by  typing 'FIND'
after a run-time error,  you can locate the statement in the source text
that caused the error.

If the compiler finds the address specified,  it stops and prints 'Found
in  line xxx'  followed  by  'Press <RETURN> to edit or <ESC> to abort'.
When  <RETURN>  is  pressed,  the  editor  is  started,  and  the cursor
automatically  moves to the place in the source text that was found.  If
the  specified  address lies  outside the program's boundaries,  'Target
address not found' is printed.

%DPolyPascal On-Line Help: DIR command.

DIR  command  is used to print a diskette's directory (library) listing.
The format of the command line is:

                          DIR <filnavn>

where  <filename>  is an ambiguous  filename of the same  format as that
used by the system's DIR  command,  i.e.,  a filename  in which question
marks (?) and asterisks ()* may appear.

A question  mark indicates that this position may contain any character,
and an asterisk  indicates that the remaining characters in the relevant
field  (the name or the type) may be anything.  If both the name and the
type  are  omitted - that  is, if  only  the drive  identifier  and  the
following  colon  are  given - the  names of all  files on the  relevant
diskette are printed.  If <filename> is completely omitted, the names of
all files on the current disk are printed.

%UPolyPascal On-Line Help: USE command.

USE command is used to activate a newly  inserted diskette and to change
the current disk or the user number.  The format of the command line is:

                        USE <disk><user> or <list>

On CP/M, <disk> is a disk identifier (A-P)  and  <user> is a user number
(0-15).  If <disk> is given,  the current disk is changed.  If <user> is
given, the user number is changed.  A USE command must be executed every
time a diskette is  changed in one of the diskette drives (otherwise you
risk  CP/M  giving  an  R/O  error  when  you attempt  to  write to  the
diskette).  The  USE command finishes  by printing the  current disk and
the current user number.

On DOS,  <list>  is a  drive identifier  and/or  a sequence of directory
names separated by backslashes.  The format is  the same as that used by
the CHDIR command in DOS.  If list is omitted,  USE prints the list that
leads to the current subdirectory.

%MPolyPascal On-Line Help: MEMORY command.

The MEMORY command prints the size of the program code and the number of
free bytes in the workspace:

              Code: rrrrrr bytes (aaaa paragraphs)
              Data: ssssss bytes (bbbb paragraphs)
              Free: tttttt bytes (cccc paragraphs)

'Code' and 'Data' lines are printed only  if a translated version of the
current  program exists in memory.  The numbers shown in parentheses are
the sizes of the respective segments in "paragraphs". The numbers are in
hex notation, and one paragraph corresponds to 16 bytes.

%ZPolyPascal On-Line Help: ZAP command.

ZAP  command  is used to  delete the text in memory or to delete a  disk
file. The format of the command line is:

                             ZAP <filnavn>

The system automatically  chooses  '.PAS'  if the file  type is omitted.
If  <filename>  is  given,  the  corresponding  disk  file  is  deleted.
If <filename> is omitted,  the text in memory is deleted and the name of
the work file  is set to 'WORK.PAS'.  If the work file has been modified
since it was loaded or last saved,  PolyPascal asks whether it should be
saved  before being deleted.  If the answer is no, the changes that were
made are lost.

%QPolyPascal On-Line Help: QUIT command.

QUIT command returns to the operating system.  If the work file has been
modified  since it was loaded or last saved,  PolyPascal asks whether it
should be  saved before  PolyPascal is exited.  If the answer is no, the
changes that were made are lost.

PolyPascal  can  later be  warm-started  with the command line *'PPAS '.
This  does, however,  require  that  no programs  have  been  run in the
meantime that have overwritten parts of the workspace.

